    {
      "id": 10,
      "type": "logs",
      "title": "Events (newest first)",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "loki",
        "uid": "loki"
      },
      "description": "One line per Suricata alert. Expand a line for every EVE field (payload metadata, flow id, HTTP headers).",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 14,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 31
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "loki",
            "uid": "loki"
          },
          "refId": "A",
          "queryType": "range",
          "expr": "{job=\"suricata\"} |~ \"(?i)$filter\" | json | event_type=\"alert\" and alert_severity <= $maxsev and (alert_signature != \"ET SCAN SYN Scan detected\" or src_ip !~ \"192\\\\.168\\\\.5\\\\.([1-9]|1[0-9]|20)\") | line_format \"sev{{.alert_severity}} {{.alert_signature}} | {{.src_ip}}:{{.src_port}} -> {{.dest_ip}}:{{.dest_port}} {{with .http_hostname}}{{.}} {{end}}{{with .http_http_method}}{{.}} {{end}}{{with .http_url}}{{.}} {{end}}{{with .http_status}}status={{.}}{{end}}\""
        }
      ],
      "options": {
        "showTime": true,
        "wrapLogMessage": true,
        "sortOrder": "Descending",
        "enableLogDetails": true,
        "dedupStrategy": "none",
        "prettifyLogMessage": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 11,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "How to read this",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 7,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 45
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "**What pages:** ET Open alerts with severity 1\u20132. SYN-scan hits from the infrastructure range 192.168.5.1\u201320 (probes, log shipping) are excluded. *Paging only* matches the alert rule exactly; *all severities* adds the INFO/POLICY noise.\n\n**What Suricata can see:** packets on ace's own NIC (`bond0`). Plain HTTP (port 80) is inspected; HTTPS (443) is encrypted, so probes that go straight to HTTPS are invisible here.\n\n**Triage:** (1) *Who* \u2014 an outside address hitting many web-exploit signatures is an internet scanner; check it on AbuseIPDB. (2) *Where* \u2014 which virtual host and port. (3) *Did it work?* \u2014 check the `status=` in the events and the app's own logs; a 3xx/4xx or an empty response means nothing was served. (4) A LAN source (192.168.x) is worth a closer look.\n\nRunbook: [IDS test cases, TC-2.2](https://serverdocs.prestonhager.com/shared/security-ids-test-cases.html) \u00b7 [IDS plan](https://serverdocs.prestonhager.com/shared/security-ids-plan.html)"
      }
    }
  ]
